<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Algiers</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Algiers is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> port. Under Ottoman rule it was the seat of a powerful regency. The French capture of Algiers in 1830 began the conquest of Algeria and the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s second colonial empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/algiers.jpg" alt="Algiers" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manini row · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_baie_d%27Alger_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Hussein Dey of the Regency of <b>Algiers</b> struck the French consul with a fly-whisk in 1827 and a naval blockade failed to bring him to terms, the government of Charles X, hoping for prestige before elections, sent an army of more than 30,000 men under General Louis…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He took Constantine and Bejaia in 1230 and <b>Algiers</b> in 1235, and in 1242 forced the Zayyanid ruler of Tlemcen, Yaghmurasan, to acknowledge his overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rainilaiarivony was exiled to <b>Algiers</b>, where he died in 1896.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Miguel de Cervantes spent five years as a captive in <b>Algiers</b> and tried to escape four times before Trinitarian friars ransomed him in 1580.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The last Wattasid, Abu Hassun, retook the city in 1554 with Ottoman troops from <b>Algiers</b>, but Muhammad al-Shaykh defeated and killed him in the same year, and the Saadis became the undisputed rulers of Morocco from the Mediterranean to the Sahara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>France seized <b>Algiers</b> in 1830 and after 1850 rebuilt a worldwide empire in North and West Africa and Indochina, which at its height in the 1920s covered over 11,500,000 km², with 110 million people on the eve of World War II.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
